When presenting your concerns to your partner, use collaborative language to avoid placing responsibility solely on them. Here are some examples of how to phrase your concerns:

* "Hey, I've been feeling overwhelmed lately, and I wanted to talk to you about it. I've tried to take some time for myself, but I'm still feeling unsure. Can we discuss what's been going on and see if we can find a way to support each other?"
* "I've been noticing that I've been feeling anxious about our relationship, and I want to make sure we're on the same page. Can we schedule a time to talk about our feelings and concerns?"
* "I'm feeling a bit lost about how to navigate this situation, and I value your perspective. Can we work together to find a solution that works for both of us?"

By using "we" language and focusing on shared problem-solving, you can present your concerns in a way that feels collaborative and avoids placing responsibility solely on your partner. Remember to listen actively to your partner's perspective and be open to finding a mutually beneficial solution.

